From 6b8d8b8af4c411de4dee6c2e40b5a1be00a7cd59 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chromium Extend <noreply@localhost>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 16:40:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 34/34] Default the toolbar shortcut to Share rather than to
 browsing habits

The toolbar shortcut defaulted to AUTO, shown in settings as "based on your usage".
That asks the segmentation platform to choose the button and to keep changing it as
browsing habits change, so the shortcut moves on its own. A shortcut that moves is one
you cannot build muscle memory for, and the behavioural segmentation it relies on is
the kind of profiling this build removes elsewhere.

Share is the most broadly useful of the fixed options, so it becomes the default.

Only the default changes. Every option including AUTO is still listed under Settings >
Appearance > Toolbar shortcut, and an explicitly chosen value is stored and takes
precedence. Because this reads through to a SharedPreferences default rather than a
cached flag, existing profiles that never made an explicit choice pick it up too.
---
 .../toolbar/adaptive/AdaptiveToolbarPrefs.java      | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/chrome/browser/ui/android/toolbar/java/src/org/chromium/chrome/browser/toolbar/adaptive/AdaptiveToolbarPrefs.java b/chrome/browser/ui/android/toolbar/java/src/org/chromium/chrome/browser/toolbar/adaptive/AdaptiveToolbarPrefs.java
index f87f803111..3f8c65a7e9 100644
--- a/chrome/browser/ui/android/toolbar/java/src/org/chromium/chrome/browser/toolbar/adaptive/AdaptiveToolbarPrefs.java
+++ b/chrome/browser/ui/android/toolbar/java/src/org/chromium/chrome/browser/toolbar/adaptive/AdaptiveToolbarPrefs.java
@@ -41,9 +41,20 @@ public class AdaptiveToolbarPrefs {
      * @return The current customization setting. See {@link AdaptiveToolbarButtonVariant}.
      */
     public static @AdaptiveToolbarButtonVariant int getCustomizationSetting() {
+        // De-Googled build: default to Share rather than AUTO.
+        //
+        // AUTO is the "based on your usage" option, which asks the segmentation platform to pick
+        // the button and change it as browsing habits change. A shortcut that moves is a shortcut
+        // you cannot build muscle memory for, and the segmentation it depends on is exactly the
+        // kind of behavioural profiling this build removes elsewhere.
+        //
+        // Share is the most broadly useful of the fixed options. This only changes the default:
+        // every choice, AUTO included, is still offered under Settings > Appearance > Toolbar
+        // shortcut, and an explicit choice is stored and wins over this.
         return ChromeSharedPreferences.getInstance()
                 .readInt(
-                        ADAPTIVE_TOOLBAR_CUSTOMIZATION_SETTINGS, AdaptiveToolbarButtonVariant.AUTO);
+                        ADAPTIVE_TOOLBAR_CUSTOMIZATION_SETTINGS,
+                        AdaptiveToolbarButtonVariant.SHARE);
     }
 
     /**
